CAT = "accafaBookkeeping"; SEG = "BKP"
def m(v): return "${:,.0f}".format(v)
Q = [
("A supplier sends a document to correct an overcharge on an invoice it issued earlier. This document is a:",
 "Credit note", ["Debit note", "Remittance advice", "Supplier statement"],
 "A credit note reduces the amount the customer owes. A debit note is sent by the customer to the supplier to request such a credit."),
("A customer sends a document with its payment showing which invoices the payment settles. This document is a:",
 "Remittance advice", ["Receipt", "Credit note", "Goods received note"],
 "A remittance advice tells the supplier what is being paid. A receipt is issued by the person who receives the money."),
("Which document does a business prepare when goods arrive from a supplier?",
 "Goods received note", ["Goods despatched note", "Purchase order", "Sales order"],
 "The goods received note records what actually arrived, so it can be matched to the purchase order and the supplier's invoice. The supplier prepares the despatch note."),
("A business has assets of $184,600 and liabilities of $57,300. Its capital is:",
 m(184600 - 57300), [m(184600 + 57300), m(184600), "$137,300"],
 "Capital = assets - liabilities = $184,600 - $57,300 = $127,300. Adding the liabilities instead gives $241,900."),
("Opening capital was $62,000. The owner introduced $8,500 and took drawings of $14,200. Closing capital was $71,900. Profit for the year was:",
 m(71900 - 62000 - 8500 + 14200), [m(71900 - 62000 - 8500), m(71900 - 62000 + 8500 + 14200), m(71900 - 62000)],
 "Closing capital = opening + capital introduced + profit - drawings, so profit = $71,900 - $62,000 - $8,500 + $14,200 = $15,600. Forgetting the drawings gives $1,400."),
("Goods are bought on credit from a supplier. The double entry is:",
 "Dr Purchases, Cr Payables", ["Dr Payables, Cr Purchases", "Dr Purchases, Cr Bank", "Dr Inventory, Cr Revenue"],
 "Purchases (an expense) increase with a debit, and the amount owed to the supplier is a credit to payables. No cash moves until the supplier is paid."),
("A credit customer returns faulty goods. The double entry is:",
 "Dr Sales returns, Cr Receivables", ["Dr Receivables, Cr Sales returns", "Dr Sales returns, Cr Bank", "Dr Purchases returns, Cr Payables"],
 "Sales returns reduce income, so they are debited, and the customer now owes less, so receivables are credited. No refund was paid, so the bank is not affected."),
("A business registered for sales tax at 20% makes a credit sale with a gross value of $5,640, tax included. The sales tax is:",
 m(5640 * 20 / 120), [m(5640 * 0.20), m(5640 * 100 / 120), m(5640 * 0.10)],
 "Tax inside a gross amount is 20/120 of it: $5,640 x 20/120 = $940. Taking 20% of the gross figure ($1,128) treats the tax as if it were on top."),
("In a quarter, sales tax on sales was $18,350 and sales tax on purchases was $11,620. The sales tax account shows:",
 "$6,730 owed to the tax authority", ["$6,730 due from the tax authority", "$29,970 owed to the tax authority", "$11,620 due from the tax authority"],
 "Output tax less input tax: $18,350 - $11,620 = $6,730. Output tax is higher, so the business owes the difference."),
("A business that is not registered for sales tax buys equipment for $3,000 plus $600 sales tax. The equipment is recorded at:",
 "$3,600", ["$3,000", "$2,400", "$600"],
 "An unregistered business cannot reclaim input tax, so the tax is part of the cost of the asset. A registered business would record $3,000 and a $600 receivable from the tax authority."),
("Goods with a list price of $8,000 are sold on credit with a 15% trade discount. The revenue recorded is:",
 m(8000 * 0.85), [m(8000), m(8000 * 0.15), m(8000 * 1.15)],
 "Trade discounts are deducted before the sale is recorded: $8,000 x 85% = $6,800. The discount is never shown as a separate expense."),
("A business sells goods for $2,500 and offers a 4% discount for payment within 10 days. It expects the customer to take the discount. Revenue recorded at the date of sale is:",
 m(2500 * 0.96), [m(2500), m(2500 * 0.04), m(2500 * 1.04)],
 "Under IFRS 15 a settlement discount is variable consideration. If the customer is expected to take it, revenue is recorded net: $2,500 x 96% = $2,400."),
("Goods were sold for $1,800 with a 5% settlement discount the customer was not expected to take. The customer then pays within the discount period. The business should:",
 "Reduce revenue by $90", ["Record an expense of $90", "Reduce revenue by $1,710", "Make no entry at all"],
 "Revenue was recorded at $1,800. When the discount is taken, revenue is reduced by 5% x $1,800 = $90 and receivables are credited, so the customer's account is cleared by $1,710 of cash."),
("A business pays a supplier $3,920 to settle an invoice of $4,000, taking a 2% settlement discount. The discount is recorded as:",
 "Income of $80", ["An expense of $80", "A reduction in revenue", "Income of $3,920"],
 "A discount received is a gain to the business: Dr Payables $4,000, Cr Bank $3,920, Cr Discounts received $80. Discounts allowed to customers, not received, reduce revenue."),
("Goods are sold for immediate payment into the bank. The double entry is:",
 "Dr Bank, Cr Revenue", ["Dr Revenue, Cr Bank", "Dr Receivables, Cr Revenue", "Dr Bank, Cr Receivables"],
 "Cash comes in, so the bank is debited, and income is earned, so revenue is credited. Receivables are only used when the customer is given credit."),
("A petty cash imprest is $250. Vouchers for the week total $173 and $77 remains in the tin. The amount needed to restore the imprest is:",
 "$173", ["$77", "$250", "$327"],
 "Under an imprest system the float is topped up by exactly what was spent, $173, which brings the tin back to $250. Topping up by the cash remaining would leave only $154."),
("A key feature of storing accounting records on external servers (the cloud) is that the records:",
 "Can be reached from any location", ["No longer need user passwords", "Can never be lost or corrupted", "No longer need a trial balance"],
 "Cloud storage gives access wherever there is an internet connection and shares backup work with the provider. Records still need security controls, and errors can still occur."),
("Which of these accounts normally has a credit balance?",
 "Payables", ["Drawings", "Purchases", "Carriage inwards"],
 "Liabilities, capital and income have credit balances. Drawings, purchases and carriage inwards are debits that reduce capital or profit."),
("A receivables account shows debits of $46,200 and credits of $39,750. When the account is balanced, the balance brought down is:",
 "$6,450 debit", ["$6,450 credit", "$85,950 debit", "$39,750 credit"],
 "Debits exceed credits by $46,200 - $39,750 = $6,450. The balance carried down sits on the credit side to make the totals agree, and is brought down as a debit, an asset."),
("The owner pays a business supplier $600 from her personal bank account. The entry in the business's books is:",
 "Dr Payables, Cr Capital", ["Dr Drawings, Cr Payables", "Dr Payables, Cr Bank", "No entry is needed"],
 "The business owes the supplier $600 less, and the owner has put $600 of value into the business, so capital rises. The business bank account was not used."),
]
